Introduction
The conventional helicopter is limited in forward flight performance by the aerodynamic lift and propulsion limitations of the main rotor. These rotor limits come from the compressibility effects on the advancing blade, as well as stall on the retreating blade. In addition, the relatively high parasitic drag of the rotor hub and other airframe components leads to a relatively poor overall lift-to-drag ratio of the helicopter. So the need for an aircraft that could combine the benefits of a vertical takeoff and landing with the high speed cruise of a fixed-wing aircraft has led to a new type of vehicles called VTOL. These systems could also fill the gap between the helicopters and the fixed wing airplane, since they have a high vertical efficiency together with a relatively low disk loading, Figure 2.1.
Figure 2.1: Power loading vs. disc loading for different VTOL vehicle types. (From [8])
In the recent years great interests and development efforts have been devoted towards the design of unconventional VTOL aircraft based on electric distributed propulsion (eVTOLs) with the aim to create a novel concept of urban air mobility to be considered as an effective alternative to ground transportation in overcrowded metropolitan areas [14]. Furthermore, United Nations states that nowadays half of the world population lives
