2.1 Rotor-Rotor Aerodynamic Interactions Challenges in eVTOL
The eVTOL configurations are characterized by the co-presence of different aerodynamic elements typical of classical aircraft layouts, such as wings, lifting rotors, and thrusting propellers. This unusual configurations pose unprecedented challenges to engineers in several area; for example the implementation of a safe and accurate controller system, the integration between different avionic systems or the achievement of stable handling qualities or low levels of noise. According to an aerodynamic standpoint, even if the eVTOL configurations developed by various companies seem rather different, they are all characterised by the presence of several electric driven rotors one near to each other, as can be seen in Figure 2.3. The rotors can be mounted on the same wing in close proximity, as in Archer aircraft, Figure 2.3a, or in Vahana designed by Airbus, Figure 2.3b. Another possibility is that the propellers are positioned in different lifting surfaces with the rotor disks overlapping in a certain region, as in the Bell-Nexus 6HX designed by Bell and shown in Figure 2.3c, or the S4 aircraft designed by Joby Aviation shown in Figure 2.3d. Therefore the aerodynamic interactions between eVTOL propellers can be divided in two categories: the side, Figure 2.4a, and tandem, Figure 2.4b. The main difference is that in the second case the propellers are overlapped and so the slipstream of the front one reaches the rear propeller disk.
