2.2 Bibliography Analysis
The above mentioned interactions could produce variations in performances of the single rotor and thus on the global vehicle. In particular, since these phenomenons are typical of new and unconventional configurations and no particular investigation has already been performed on a vehicle propeller layout typical of eVTOLs, the starting point of the analysis can be identified in the numerous researches that have been conducted on small unmanned drones equipped with a quadrotor system. In fact, such flying systems are produced by many commercial companies and in several different configurations, thus the analysis of the related rotor aerodynamics have been of great interest in the scientific community in the past years. In fact, Zhou [21] performed a series of wind tunnel experiments for two identical rotors in side configuration. In particular, the tested blade was the one of the DJI Phantom II since it is easily available in commerce, and its performances were measured for a rotational speed of 4860 RPM. First of all, he investigated the isolated rotor to set a reference value for the developed thrust coefficient and then the lateral separation distance between the blade tips of two counter rotating rotors was changed from 0.05D to 1D. The comparison in the developed thrust coefficient between the single rotor and the side configuration showed that the generated mean thrust was independent of the separation distance, since the deficit of CT at the lower gap was only of around 2%. Instead the thrust fluctuations were found to increase dramatically as the lateral separation became smaller and eventually equal to three times the fluctuations of the isolated rotor when the blade tips distance was 0.05D. Furthermore he performed also a series of PIV (Particle Image Velocimetry) measurements for both the average velocity field and the vorticity field for the layout of side rotors at lateral blade tip separation of 0.05D. The first one showed that the slipstream for the side interaction was found to be slightly dragged towards the adjacent rotor if compared to the single rotor case, as shown in Figure 2.5. Instead, the vorticity field indicated that the tip vortices merged and dissipated much faster for the side interaction than the single rotor case, as shown in Figure 2.6. Shukla and Komerath [17] set up a similar experiments, thus considering the side rotor interaction of two minidrones. They found that while reducing the relative distance between the rotors, the Figure of Merit (FM), which is a measure of efficiency, slowly dropped and so the overall performances. Furthermore, through PIV meaurements they showed that decreasing the rotor distance, the tip vortices deviated from the single rotor
14


trajectory and they also lost their coherent structures in about one rotation. They also demonstrated that the same configuration, but for lower Reynolds number, showed more interaction effects since the tip vortices were weaker in terms of circulation. In addition, Shukla and Komerath [16] investigated also the coaxial configuration. In this layout one rotor is over the other one which is invested by the wake and so, due to the interaction, it should experience different performances. In particular, through a series of wind tunnel tests they showed that the upper rotor produced higher values of FM for all the considered Reynolds numbers if compared to the isolated one. Instead, since the lower rotor operated in higher downwash, its FM was found to be lower. Moreover, they obtained a series of PIV measurements for the coaxial interaction. Results showed that tip vortices of the lower rotor didn’t follow a fixed path, but they tended to alternate two possible trajectories. Instead, the upper rotor vortices stayed on a fixed line because they had higher strengths and convection velocities. A series of numerical simulations were performed by Alvarez and Ning [2] through the use of a mid-fidelity aerodynamic code based on VPM (Vortex Particle Method). In particular, three different experimental studies were reproduced in order to validate the code. The first one reflected the configuration analysed by Zhou, i.e. the DJI Phantom II blade isolated and at 4860 RPM. The computed mean thrust coefficient was found to have 0.5% error with respect to the experimental one. Moreover, the obtained wake showed the topology typical of a hovering rotor: a well defined structure near the rotor disk, leapfrog (i.e. interaction of pair of tip vortices) at a downstream distance of 0.5D and the transition to a turbulent breakdown at 1D. The second simulated configuration was a APC thin-electric propeller in forward flight. The observed thrust and torque coefficients was in great agreement with the experimental results performed by McCrink [11] for low and moderate advance ratios, J, instead they were underestimated at high advancing speeds. This reflected in the propulsive efficiency which was well predicted until J = 0.725. In the end he numerical simulated also the side interaction previously investigated by Zhou. The obtained mean thrust coefficient at a blade tip separation distance of 0.05D was found to be 3% lower than the isolated rotor case, thus confirming the validity of the VPM code for side interaction investigation. In addition, the obtained vorticity field showed that, as predicted by the wind tunnel tests, the tip vortices lost their structures nearer to the rotor disk than for the isolated case, in particular at a downstream distance of 0.4D they were not recognizable anymore, and the resulting fields for single and side rotors were found to be similar to the ones of Zhou, as presented in Figure 2.7. The only work which deals with a VTOL vehicle is the one by Yoon [23]. He performed a series of CFD simulations on a typical full vehicle configuration of a quadrotor VTOL. He found that the developed thrust coefficient of rotor in side configuration at a distance of 0.1D was 4% lower. In addition, the interactions of their wakes with wings and fuselage reduced the global vehicle lift of 16%. The interaction between rotors and fuselage has been studied also by Yoon and Diaz [15]. They performed a series of high-fidelity CFD simulations on different drones configurations. In particular, they examined the DJI Phantom III drone in which the positions of the four rotors were varied in relation to the fuselage: overmount, undermount and off-body undermount, as in Figure 2.8. They found that the first layout guaranteed the highest downwash and so the biggest total thrust. Instead, the other two configurations, due to the interaction of the flow with the drone body, developed respectively 1% and 2% less of total thrust. Instead, analysing the isolated rotor, they concluded that the developed thrust in off-body configuration was 6% lower than the one for the undermount and 7% smaller with respect to the overmount. Moreover, they showed that huge advantages could be produced mounting the propellers both above and below arms in coaxial configuration, as in Figure 2.9. In the end, even if the interest in eVTOL is huge, there is a lack in literature in the study of aerodynamic interaction for rotor configurations, both geometrical position and flight conditions, typical of these kind of vehicles.
