2.3 Objectives of the Work
As demonstrated in the two previous sections, eVTOL vehicles are a new and potentially profitable reality, but in spite of that, the research in the field of aerodynamic rotor interactions typical of such configurations is, until now, very limited. Some panel and filament methods have been developed for the modeling of unsteady free wakes, but the obtained results show that the preservation of wake connectivity makes such methods ill-conditioned for the modeling of wake mixing [9]. A possible way to investigate this topic is through the computational fluid dynamic (CFD). However a high-fidelity method, still requires too much computational effort for the simulation of a complex system like an eVTOL in which there are a lot of lifting elements rotating and that can mutually influence. On the other hand, a mid-fidelity numerical approach characterized by low computational effort, seems to be the best compromise to simulate both different types of rotor-rotor interactions and a wide range of space parameters. In particular, the use of vortex particle method (VPM) [5, 22] for wake modelling enables to obtain a better representation of the rotor aerodynamic characteristics and to capture the aerodynamic interactions between several bodies. In fact, as shown in the previous section, Alvarez and Ning [2] used a midfidelity aerodynamic code based on VPM to simulate numerical experiments of side propellers, thus obtaining good results. DUST (https://www.dust-project.org/) is a new medium fidelity aerodynamic open-source software developed by Politecnico di Milano in collaboration with A3 by Airbus LLC. It has been specifically realized for the simulation of complex aircraft configurations in a fast and reliable way. To do that, the adopted methods are lifting lines, vortex lattices and surface panels for the computation of loads, instead to accurately model the aerodynamic wake interactions a vortex particle method is accelerated through a Fast Multipole Method (FMM) [10]. DUST can be considered a reliable tool since it is already been tested on different rotorcraft configurations and the obtained results are in great agreement with reference CFD simulations. Furthermore, the mid-fidelity simulation have been demonstrated to properly predict complex aerodynamic multi-rotor configurations [6]. The aim of the following work is to investigate and comprehend the typical aerodynamic rotor-rotor interactions proper of an eVTOL vehicle. This reflects in both the choice of the mutual positions of the propellers and in the flight conditions. In particular, as shown in Figure 2.3, the two most significant aerodynamic interactions are the side and tandem ones. Since it is a relatively unapproached field, a particular focus is posed on the second one, thus considering different lateral and longitudinal distances between the rotors. The research is centered on the phase of cruise flight, since is the simpler and also the one in which possible improvement on the aerodynamic characteristics of such vehicles, may lead to huge advantages in the design and competitiveness of eVTOLs. To obtain valuable results, a series of numerical simulations are performed through the presented mid-fidelity software, DUST, and they will eventually be confirmed with future wind tunnels tests. The importance of this research is that, until now, none literature work has completely and deeply investigated these types of rotors interactions. In addition, since the eVTOL vehicles are powered by an electrical system, a possible improvement in their aerodynamic can produce an increase in the flights range.
