3.2 Test Model
Two propellers have been designed and produced for the wind tunnel tests. Each of these models is composed by a three bladed VarioPropeller 12C which has a diameter of 300mm and it is mounted on 65 mm diameter aluminium spinner. The main advantage of using such propellers is that the rotor hub has a internal system that permits a fine adjustment of the collective blade pitch, and thus the same model can be used for a wide range of experimental conditions. Inside the manufactured nacelle, which is made in polycarbonate through a FDM technique, there is a aluminium frame to support the driving system and the measurement sensors. The propeller is driven by a brushless motor equipped with a Hall effect sensor that ensures a signal for each revolution for measure the rotational speed. The sensor for the measurement of developed propeller thrust and torque consists of a Futek MBA500 strain gauge bi-axial load cell (50 lbs F.S.). The resulting internal and external layouts of the model are shown in Figure 3.2. The two propellers are mounted inside the test chamber on a thin metallic strut that allows the modification of their distances, in both lateral and longitudinal directions, enabling the analysis of both side and tandem rotor configurations, as shown in Figure 3.3.
3.3 Measurement Techniques
To measure the thrust and torque developed by the tested rotors in different configurations, a Futek MBA500 bi-axial strain gauge load cell is selected. The main advantages are that it is light and compact so it can be easily placed inside the model, and it also ensures high quality measurements since two separate outputs for applied torque and thrust forces are present and it also ensures a 0.2% nonlinearity error and yet provide 150% (50 to 150 lb) 125% ((200 lb) over-capacity. To capture the flow field which results from both the single rotor and interactional configurations, a Particle Image Velocimetry (PIV) survey is planned. The PIV system is constituted by a Litron NANO-L-200-15 Nd:Yag double-pulse laser with an output energy of 200 mJ and wavelength of 532 nm, and two Imperx ICLB1921M CCD cameras with a 12-bit, 1952 x 1112 pixel array. The laser is positioned under the floor of the wind tunnel test section to provide a laser sheet aligned with the freestream direction. The cameras are mounted on a metallic strut attached to one of the lateral wall of the test section and are positioned in tandem to cover the area of interest of the surveys. In this test campaign phase-locked 2C measurements are planned for different azimuthal angles of the blades. The flow fields are then time averaged to evaluate the mean behaviour of the interacting wakes of the rotors and phase averaged in order to investigate the evolution in time of the vortices released by the blades and their interactions. For the side-by-side configuration the area of interest is the region between the rotor hubs past the rotors disks, while for the tandem con-figuration the two cameras are used simultaneously to investigate the flow regions before and past the rear propeller disk.
