3.4 Test Conditions and Parameters
The operating conditions of the test can be selected by modifying different parameters. For the selected wind tunnel, the pressure, p∞, and air density, ρ∞, cannot be controlled and thus they are the same as the external atmospheric conditions. Instead the operator can modify: the freestream velocity, the rotational speed of the propellers and their lateral and longitudinal distances. First of all, a series of tests are performed on the isolated propeller. These are conducted varying the rotational speed between 1000 and 12000 RPM and each one through different freestream velocity from 16 m/s to 35 m/s. This permits to obtain a full reference matrix across several advance ratios and disk loadings, and it will be used to compare the results of the propeller interactions. After that the side interaction is investigated. Since the aim of the work is to understand the resulting aerodynamic performances of an eVTOL in cruise condition, the parallelism between the wind tunnel experiments and the real conditions is obtained considering to reach the same Mach number at the tip blades. In particular, the model rotational speed is considered to be fixed at 7000 RPM, so that for the maximum freestream velocity of 35 m/s the tip Mach number will be of around 0.5 (the same of a cruising eVTOL). As a result, the side interaction tests are performed for 7000 RPM for different advance ratios and also changing the lateral separation distance between the rotor hubs from 2.05 to 3 rotor radii. In the end, the tandem interaction is studied considering the same conditions at the blade tip. So the tests are performed for a rotational speed of 7000 RPM across different advance ratios, obtained varying the freestream velocity between 16 m/s to 35 m/s. For this type of interaction two possible distances can be changed: the longitudinal, dz, and lateral, dy. For the first one, to understand how this length influence the performance, two possible values have been selected. In particular looki