Structure and Formulation of the
Numerical Code
The code is mainly composed by a pre-processor, solver and a post-processor and the typical DUST adopted process is shown in Figure 4.1. In the pre-processor the geometry components are or imported from pre-existing meshes or created parametrically. The volume mesh must not be defined, since the solid bodies are modeled as a surface distribution of singularities. Each frame is considered as an aerodynamic element which can be computed with a different level of fidelity: from one dimensional lifting line and actuator disk to zero thickness lifting surfaces and three-dimensional surface panels. The complete geometry model is created associating at each aerodynamic element a local reference frame. This is then related to the global and unique reference system, so that each component has a specific position in the considered volume. The solver needs as input the mesh geometries together with their reference frames and relative positions, the simulation conditions and the numerical solver parameters. The solution consists of a series of singularities with different intensities, which are used to represent both the solid bodies and the resulting wakes. The intensity of these singularities is determined as the solution of the boundary value problem for the potential part of the velocity, supplied with the non-penetration boundary conditions. The different solutions provided by the solver are used as input to the post-processor, which can reconstruct some useful quantities. First of all, from the integration of the pressure distribution, the loads acting on each aerodynamic element can be computed. Furthermore, also the visualisation of both average and instantaneous velocity fields can be obtained in a desired control volume.
The aerodynamic solver relies on the Helmholtz decomposition of the velocity field, ~u = ~uφ + ~uψ, being ~uφ = ∇φ and ~uψ = ∇ × ψ the irrotational and solenoidal contributions respectively, with φ representing the kinetic potential and ψ the vector potential. So the aerodynamic problem is rearranged as the composition of two sub-problems, one for the irrotational velocity ~uφ and one for rotational contribution ~uψ. Using the intrinsic proprieties of such velocity components, this two problems can be rearranged and solved separately. Then they are summed up in order to obtain the resulting velocity field. Since the rotational velocity is a solenoidal vector field, the incompressibility constraint reduces to the Laplace equation for the kinetic potential φ~(r, t).
