In the following chapter some preliminary calculations are performed in order to validate the parameters used for DUST simulations, which will be adopted for the same propeller selected also for wind tunnel tests. First in Section 5.1 the DJI blade in literature hover conditions is analysed. Then in Section 5.2 the forward flight phase is simulated on the APC blade. In the end, in Section 5.3 the literature side interaction between DJI propellers is repeated in order to obtain the simulation parameters useful for the analysis of following propeller interactions.
5.1 Hover Simulation with DJI Blade
First of all, some simulations are performed on the blade of DJI Phantom II to validate the choice of simulation parameters against experiments. In fact, Zhou [21] performs a series of wind tunnel tests with this rotor in hover condition at a rotational speed of 4860 RPM. The resulting average thrust coefficient has a value equal to 0.013. Furthermore, Alvarez [2] conducts a series of numerical simulations in the same conditions of Zhou and through a vortex particle code. He finds out a thrust coefficient only 0.5% different from the experimental one. As a result, in this work CT = 0.013 is assumed to be the reference value of thrust coefficient developed by the DJI Phantom II Blade in such conditions. A spatial and temporal convergence study is performed with DUST against this experiment.
5.1.1 DJI Geometry Definition
The DJI blade mesh, useful for DUST solver, is created according to the geometry reported in Ning’s [21] and Alvarez’s [2] papers. In fact, this rotor is 240mm in diameter, 11mm in tip chord and its geometry approximates the optimum rotor design obtained by imposing a constant effective angle of attack along the blade. Thus, it is digitized assuming the twist distribution θ(r) = 5.3◦ + 6.3◦/r (with r the radial position) and a chord distribution c(r) = ctip/r. Since these distributions don’t permit to obtain acceptable values near the blade root, linear interpolations are used in the region 0 < r < 0.3, which is the first 30% of the blade, for both chord and twist. This procedure is also adopted in the two literature articles. The resulting distributions are shown in Figure 5.1. An E63 airfoil, which has an high ratio Cl/Cd, is assumed for each blade section. According to what is stated in Section 4.1, the aerodynamic tables are generated using XFoil. In particular, the airfoil is defined by points and the simulations are performed using 200 as value for both the number of panels and iterations, and changing the angle of attack between -12◦ and 12◦, with viscous simulations. The main objective of the analysis of the DJI blade is to reproduce the results presented by Zhou [21], whose experiments are performed in hover
