Chapter 9
Conclusions and Recommendations
In this chapter the main conclusions obtained from this work are presented in Section 9.1. After that, in Section 9.2, recommendations for possible future researches are described.
9.1 Conclusions
Numerical and experimental activities have been performed in order to investigate the rotor-rotor aerodynamic interactions typical of eVTOL vehicles. In particular, the selected configurations have been the side and tandem ones. The test matrix and the model setups useful for future wind tunnel tests have been described and designed. After that, the numerical simulations have been performed through a mid-fidelity aerodynamic solver: DUST, and the program, responsible for the calculation of the aerodynamic coefficients useful for the lifting line method, has been implemented and presented. The low computational effort, guaranteed by DUST, has permitted to model and simulate a wide range of propeller configurations and flight conditions typical of an eVTOL. First of all, some literature results on rotor-rotor aerodynamic interactions have been repeated, in order to verify the suitability of DUST software for this kind of investigations and to find the most proper simulation parameters. In particular, the DJI Phantom II blade in hover at the same conditions presented by Zhou [21] has been modeled and simulated. The numerical parameters which permits to achieve an error of only 0.007% on the mean thrust coefficient with respect to the literature one, results to be: 10 revolutions of 72 time intervals each and with 21 elements in span for each blade. The forward flight condition of a propeller has been simulated through the APC 10x7 thin-electric blade at the same conditions of McCrink wind tunnel experiments. The used numerical parameters are: 10 revolutions of 72 time intervals each and with 41 elements in span, which produces a error on CT at advance ratio of 0.6 equal to 2.5%. The last literature configuration analysed is two counter rotating DJI Phantom II blades in hover at a blade tip separation distance of 0.05D. The obtained mean thrust coefficient is found to be 2.1% lower with respect to the isolated rotor case. Furthermore, in the computed average velocity field, the wake of the rotor results to be dragged towards the adjacent one and the vorticity field shows that the side interaction produces a faster dissipation of tip vortices. All the presented results, being quite similar to the respective literature ones, demonstrate that DUST in conjunction with the considered numerical parameters, are an appropriate tool to simulate the rotor-rotor aerodynamic interaction. After that, the isolated propeller, which is the same that has been selected also for wind tunnel tests, has been modeled and simulated in order to set reference parameters for the comparison with rotor interactions results. In all the simulations, the focus has been posed on the cruise flight phase of an eVTOL and since these vehicles are designed to fly in urban areas, their cruise speed is around 100 km/h (28 m/s). So, in order to obtain the maximum propulsive efficiency in these conditions, which traduces in an advance ratio of J = 0.8, the blade
87


collective pitch has been set to 25.5◦.
The side interaction has been investigated varying the lateral separation distance between the rotor tips, while being in forward flight at an advance ratio equal to 0.8. Results show that the performances reduce as the separation distance decreases. However, such effect can be considered negligible since at the lower considered distance of 0.05R, the deficit in developed thrust coefficient is around 1% with respect to the isolated configuration. Instead, the fluctuations in CT are relevant and they have been lead back to huge changes of effective angle of attack produced in a small portion of revolution. In particular, the lower effective angles of attack can be identified in the azimuthal angle range between 300◦ and 330◦, instead the higher around 0◦. Through the analysis of the average flow fields for the smallest separation distance and at J = 0.8, a region of accelerated wake can be recognised between the two rotor disks at a downstream distance of 0.5R. Such effect has been explained to be caused by the merging of the two rotor wakes. Furthermore, analysing the instantaneous vorticity fields, the tip vortices are found to interact and to dissipate much faster for the side configuration than the isolated rotor one. In addition, none noticeable difference is found in the developed performance between rotors with or without phase delay. As a result, it can be concluded that the obtained performances in side propeller configuration are produced by the interaction of tip vortices shed by the two adjacent rotors. Since such vortices have equal strength but opposite sign, they dissipate and the interaction produces a region of higher angles of attack which has little influence in the developed thrust coefficient but which produces great CT fluctuations.
The tandem interaction has been investigated evaluating the changes in rear propeller performances due to the variation of lateral, dy, and longitudinal, dz distances and at different advance ratios. Particular attention is posed on the cruise flight condition of an eVTOL and so on the advance ratio equal to 0.8. As dy decreases, the rear propeller performances are find to be more influenced by the tandem interaction and in particular the developed CT reduces. In fact, at dy = 2R the rear propeller is not invested by the front rotor slipstream and so it behaves like in free stream conditions. Instead, at dy = 0R it is completely overlapped by the front rotor and the developed mean thrust coefficient is much lower and eventually around the 55% of the isolated case. Furthermore, also the dz distance influences the performances. In particular, for the bigger longitudinal distance the measured CT and CP are lower if compared to the nearer configuration. Such behaviours have been lead back to the particular distributions of axial and tangential velocities on the rear propeller and which are induced by the front rotor slipstream. In particular, when the two propellers are completely overlapped the composition of such velocities produces a global reduction of effective angles of attack in a revolution and this results in a big reduction of CT but low fluctuations. Instead, when dy is equal to 1R the tandem interaction influences only the azimuthal angles between 210◦ < ψ < 240◦ and 300◦ < ψ < 330◦. So the variation in effective angles of attack are much more local and so the deficit in thrust coefficient is lower but with higher fluctuations. Furthermore, analysing the resulting average velocity fields, the particular distributions of axial and tangential velocities are demonstrated to be produced by the acceleration of the front propeller slipstream on the rear nacelle. In conclusion, the measured performances in tandem interaction can be produced by the particular distribution of axial and tangential velocities induced by the front rotor wake on the downstream propeller.
88


9.2 Recommendations
First of all, due to Covid-19 sanitary emergency the planned wind tunnel tests have undergo to huge delays and they can’t be performed in the deadline of this thesis. So they should be conducted when it will be possible to validate the numerical results. Also the wind tunnel tests on tandem interaction could produce decreased performances as dy and dz distances increase. Even if the developed performances of the rotors produced by the interactions have been studied studied in deep, some more simulations can be performed. In particular, more longitudinal distances in the tandem configuration can be investigated. Furthermore, more than two interacting propellers can be simulated. In fact, the interaction of more than two wake propellers can produce a further deficit in the obtained thrust and power coefficients and so this may determine a lower efficiency aerodynamic configuration. In addition, also the interaction of the propellers with lifting elements like wings or with the eVTOL fuselage can be simulated. In fact, such configuration can determine increased performances of the rotors due to an apparent ground effect, but the overall result may be a global reduction of the vehicle thrust. In this thesis only the cruise flight phase of eVTOL has been taken in consideration, but also other conditions like lift off or taxing can be simulated. In fact, a full comprehension of the aerodynamic of such vehicles can be a useful starting point for the research for the most proper urban areas that may be dedicated to the eVTOL maneuvering. One more possible research in the field of the aerodynamic of interacting propellers can be the analysis of the acoustic noises produced by eVTOL propeller configurations. In fact, such analysis can help in increasing the passenger comfort but also in the reduction of the ground noise.
